  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nUpper troughing currently extends from the Northern Plains down to \nthe Southern Plains with ridging building into the western CONUS. At \nthe surface, expansive high pressure sits over the Great Lakes with \nsurface ridging extending down through the Middle Mississippi Valley \ninto the Central and Southern Plains. In our area, cooler conditions \nremain in place with below normal temperatures for this time of \nyear. High temperatures will top out in the lower 40s with light \nnortheasterly winds continuing. \n\nAs we move into tonight and Wednesday morning, the upper trough over \nthe Plains is progged to become an upper low over portions of the \nPlains and the Mississippi Valley. It is possible for some very \nlight precipitation/snow flurries to develop with this, though it \nseems more likely that it will stay north/northeast of our area and \njust clip northeast KS if anything. Guidance suggests the column \nshould remain dry over our area which again will keep these chances \nvery low. Early Wednesday morning will be chilly as lows are \nforecast to be in the lower 20s with another cool day across the \nregion as high temperatures rise into the lower 40s. For Thursday, \nthe aforementioned upper low will propagate south towards the Lower \nMississippi Valley and the Tennessee Valley, with precipitation \nchances staying well east of the area in Missouri. High temperatures \nwill be in the mid 40s for Thanksgiving with light winds and mostly \nsunny skies across our area.  \n\nAfter Thursday, weak ridging will build into the region, shifting \nflow aloft to northwesterly. Thickness is progged to increase, \nresulting in a warming trend for Friday and into the weekend. High \ntemperatures will rise back into the 50s with low temperatures \ndropping into the 30s. As we get to Saturday night, there are some \nmodel discrepancies as the ECMWF brings a shortwave trough over the \nregion with precipitation chances vs the GFS keeps the region dry. \nAt this time, chances are generally below 25%, though if any \nprecipitation were to form in our area, it would be rain showers or \nsprinkles. There is still high uncertainty with this, so continue to \nstay tuned as we refine this forecast. Outside of these chances, dry \nconditions are forecast through the weekend.",
